{
  "dom_id": "step-q143675",
  "name": "Question 13",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13943521/assets/a8fbbaff1139a0c80c5dc97a9d99a08e238a61ab65251a158b382e208be7e842.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}\u2061(4x+C),$ where $C$ is a constant. Given that $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ what is the value of $C?$",
  "worked_solution": "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{cot}\u2061(4x+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}\u2061(4(x+\\frac{C}{4}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+\\frac{C}{4}$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cot}\u2061(4(x+\\frac{C}{4}))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=\\operatorname{cot}\u2061(4x).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13943521/assets/4c5147d95cc4d5df1d847f5fe9f580d9595d0f4d8c2c8209efa1f2a3cd9c5aea.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{cot}\u2061(4x)$ by $\\frac{9\\pi }{40}-\\frac{\\pi }{8}=\\frac{\\pi }{10}$ units to the right. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\frac{C}{4}=-\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\,\u27f9\\,C=-\\frac{2\\pi }{5}\n$$",
